NNAAMMEE
     ccttff — Compact C Type Format

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ##iinncclluuddee <<ssyyss//ccttff..hh>>

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     ccttff is designed to be a compact representation of the C programming lan‐
     guage's type information focused on serving the needs of dynamic tracing,
     debuggers, and other in-situ and post-mortem introspection tools.  ccttff
     data is generally included in EELLFF objects and is tagged as SSHHTT__PPRROOGGBBIITTSS
     to ensure that the data is accessible in a running process and in subse‐
     quent core dumps, if generated.

     The ccttff data contained in each file has information about the layout and
     sizes of C types, including intrinsic types, enumerations, structures,
     typedefs, and unions, that are used by the corresponding EELLFF object.  The
     ccttff data may also include information about the types of global objects
     and the return type and arguments of functions in the symbol table.

     Because a ccttff file is often embedded inside a file, rather than being a
     standalone file itself, it may also be referred to as a ccttff ccoonnttaaiinneerr.

     On FreeBSD systems, ccttff data is consumed by dtrace(1).  Programmatic ac‐
     cess to ccttff data can be obtained through libctf.

     The ccttff file format is broken down into seven different sections.  The
     first two sections are the pprreeaammbbllee and hheeaaddeerr, which describe the ver‐
     sion of the ccttff file, the links it has to other ccttff files, and the sizes
     of the other sections.  The next section is the llaabbeell section, which pro‐
     vides a way of identifying similar groups of ccttff data across multiple
     files.  This is followed by the oobbjjeecctt information section, which de‐
     scribes the types of global symbols.  The subsequent section is the
     ffuunnccttiioonn information section, which describes the return types and argu‐
     ments of functions.  The next section is the ttyyppee information section,
     which describes the format and layout of the C types themselves, and fi‐
     nally the last section is the ssttrriinngg section, which contains the names of
     types, enumerations, members, and labels.

     While strictly speaking, only the pprreeaammbbllee and hheeaaddeerr are required, to be
     actually useful, both the type and string sections are necessary.

     A ccttff file may contain all of the type information that it requires, or
     it may optionally refer to another ccttff file which holds the remaining
     types.  When a ccttff file refers to another file, it is called the cchhiilldd
     and the file it refers to is called the ppaarreenntt.  A given file may only
     refer to one parent.  This process is called _u_n_i_q_u_i_f_i_c_a_t_i_o_n because it
     ensures each child only has type information that is unique to it.  A
     common example of this is that most kernel modules in illumos are uniqui‐
     fied against the kernel module ggeennuunniixx and the type information that
     comes from the IIPP module.  This means that a module only has types that
     are unique to itself and the most common types in the kernel are not du‐
     plicated.  Uniquification is not used when building kernel modules on
     FreeBSD.

